Q: What are SOLID principles?
	SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles that help us write code that is maintainable, flexible, testable, and loosely coupled.
	1. S — Single Responsibility Principle
		A class should have one reason to change.
		Instead of :
			class OrderService {
				void createOrder() {}
				void sendEmail() {}
				void generateInvoice() {}
			}
		Better :
			OrderService
			EmailService
			InvoiceService

	2. O — Open/Closed Principle
		A class should be open for extension but closed for modification.
		For example, instead of modifying existing payment logic every time a new payment method is added, we can use an interface:
			interface Payment {
				void pay();
			}

			class CardPayment implements Payment {
				public void pay() {}
			}

			class UPIPayment implements Payment {
				public void pay() {}
			}

	3. L — Liskov Substitution Principle
		A child class should be replaceable for its parent without breaking the application. A child class should be usable wherever
		its parent class is expected, without breaking the program.
		Example: if Bird has a fly() method, making Penguin extends Bird can violate LSP because penguins cannot fly.

	4. I — Interface Segregation Principle
		A class should not be forced to implement methods it doesn't need.
		Instead of one large interface: Split it into smaller interfaces where appropriate.
			interface Loan {
				void homeLoan();
				void carLoan();
			}
			Segregate to : CarLoan, HomeLoan, BusinessLoan
			class LoanService implements CarLoan, HomeLoan { }

	5. D — Dependency Inversion Principle
		High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.
		Spring's dependency injection is commonly used to achieve this:
			@Service
			class OrderService {

				private final PaymentService paymentService;

				OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
					this.paymentService = paymentService;
				}
			}
