Q: Explain Internal Working of @Transactional
	@Transactional works using AOP proxies that start, commit, or rollback transactions around method execution.
	When Spring sees:
		@Transactional
		public void transferMoney() {
		}
	it does not call the method directly.
	Spring creates a Proxy Object around the bean which will begin, commit or rollback the transaction.

	Execution flow :
		Client
		   |
		   v
		Spring Proxy
		   |
		Begin Transaction
		   |
		Actual Method
		   |
		Commit/Rollback

	Success Scenario:	COMMIT
	Failure Scenario: throw new RuntimeException();		ROLLBACK

	By default rollback happens for: RuntimeException, ERROR

	Not for checked exceptions.
	For checked exceptions:
		@Transactional(
			rollbackFor = Exception.class		Proxy is bypassed. Hence transaction may not work.
		)
