Q: On which Microservice Patterns you have worked ?
	1. API Gateway Pattern
		Single entry point for all client requests.
		Handled routing, authentication, logging and rate limiting.

	2. Database per Service Pattern
		Each microservice owned its own database.
		Customer Service, Account Service and Transaction Service had separate schemas/databases.
		Avoided direct database sharing between services.

	3. Circuit Breaker Pattern
		Used Resilience4j.
		Prevented cascading failures when dependent services were unavailable.

	4. CQRS Pattern
		Separated command and query responsibilities.
		Different services/components handled writes and reads independently.

	5. Event-Driven Communication
		Used Kafka for asynchronous communication between services.
		This reduced tight coupling between services.

	6. Saga Pattern
		For distributed transactions across multiple services.
		Used compensating transactions instead of traditional database transactions.

	7. Service Discovery Pattern
		Used Eureka Server.
		Services registered themselves dynamically.
		registered with Eureka and were discovered by service name instead of hardcoded URLs.
